.TH EVENT_LOOP_CREATE_WITH_MPROCS 3 "2026-07-12" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
event_loop_create_with_mprocs \- create a persistent epoll-based event loop
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <cthreadcomm.h>

event_loop event_loop_create_with_mprocs(size_t max_events_per_wait,
                        ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mmgmt_procs, char **err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a persistent epoll(7) instance and immediately spawns the single
background reactor thread that drives it.  Unlike ccol_select(), which
creates a fresh epoll instance on every call and tears it down after
exactly one ready selectable, an event_loop's epoll instance and reactor
thread live for as long as the handle does, and registrations
(event_loop_add / event_loop_modify / event_loop_remove) are mutated
incrementally against it.

The reactor thread is ready to dispatch events as soon as this call
returns, mirroring create_cthread_pool()'s "ready to work the moment
you get the handle" ergonomics.

Registrations are built from the same ccol_selectable type ccol_select
uses (selectable_from_fd, selectable_from_circq, selectable_from_dynq,
selectable_from_chan).  One deliberate difference from ccol_select: for
fd selectables, event_loop never reads the fd itself, so
selectable_from_fd_limited (a non-zero max_fd_read_bytes) is rejected by
event_loop_add.  See event_loop_add(3) for the full rationale.

Internally, one persistent epoll interest-list entry backs each
registered fd (both directions share one entry, since epoll_ctl keys its
interest list by fd, not by (fd, direction) pair), while each queue or
channel selectable gets its own dedicated bridge eventfd(2) permanently
linked into the queue's own waiter list; not the transient, per-call
waiter nodes ccol_select() uses.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_events_per_wait  Size of the epoll_wait batch buffer (must be >= 1).
mmgmt_procs          Custom memory management procedures, or NULL for
                      default malloc/free.
err_str               Optional pointer to receive an error string on
                      failure (pass NULL to ignore).
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new event_loop handle on success.
Returns NULL on failure: max_events_per_wait is 0, mmgmt_procs is
malformed (some but not all of malloc/calloc/realloc/free set),
allocation failure, or epoll_create1(2)/eventfd(2)/pthread_create(3)
failure.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Multiple independent event_loop instances may coexist in the same
process; each owns its own epoll instance and reactor thread and shares
no global state with any other instance.
.fi
.SH THREAD SAFETY
.nf
Safe to call concurrently with unrelated event_loop instances.  Not
meaningful to call concurrently "on" a handle that does not exist yet.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR event_loop_create (3),
.BR event_loop_add (3),
.BR event_loop_shutdown (3),
.BR event_loop_destroy (3)
